And so you take the the lessons that are in there and you pass them out.

731
01:07:05,806 --> 01:07:09,892
I also think that most organizations need to gather around a common set of skills.

732
01:07:10,160 --> 01:07:17,760
You need a common software workflow process so that everyone can contribute back to it, so that you can experiment with things, you can A-B test things.

733
01:07:18,094 --> 01:07:23,588
You know, you can have one team doing one set of stuff and one team doing another set of stuff, and then you ask them afterwards.

734
01:07:23,950 --> 01:07:30,000
And so everyone working with agents in any kind of organization needs this experimental mindset.

735
01:07:30,240 --> 01:07:34,436
You need to be thinking, how do we get more juice out of these tokens that we're spending?

736
01:07:34,880 --> 01:07:37,040
And observability is the first step there.

737
01:07:37,422 --> 01:07:47,760
Yeah. And I also wonder if there's a human feedback loop in the sense that I mean, just talk to your colleagues like on on like, you know, we do have rituals, team meetings,

738
01:07:48,110 --> 01:07:54,640
company-wide meetings for a reason. Like there, share, here's what's working for me, here's where it didn't work, here's what I'm learning.

739
01:07:54,800 --> 01:08:04,000
Like in the end, we are in charge of setting up the rules, deciding how we use them, where we use them, where we don't use them, and where we say, like, no, this this needs to be

740
01:08:04,160 --> 01:08:05,200
humans need to take 100%.

741
01:08:05,520 --> 01:08:08,560
Like, we're not even getting AI involved, which again will be different everywhere.

742
01:08:08,880 --> 01:08:10,020
And it's not only that, like

743
01:08:11,054 --> 01:08:14,468
a lot of this stuff now you don't need to be human in the loop for, right?

744
01:08:14,640 --> 01:08:19,840
You don't actually need to delegate that much time in order to build up a better code base.

745
01:08:20,080 --> 01:08:28,800
I have loops that essentially every morning it will run my improved code base architecture skill and give me a proposal for the something that I could improve in the code

746
01:08:28,960 --> 01:08:29,120
base.

747
01:08:29,326 --> 01:08:33,924
And then I can just press a button, I can say, okay, turn that into tickets, and then let's ship that.

748
01:08:34,240 --> 01:08:38,400
That is pretty easy to do. And it's pretty easy to stream that in with other work.

749
01:08:38,560 --> 01:08:48,240
And so I think that I don't know whether you need like 20% of your time focusing on the factory that builds your software as well as the software, because I feel like that's a massive,

750
01:08:48,400 --> 01:08:57,760
incredible investment into your future leverage and not only your leverage with your work, but also your team's leverage and understanding and getting better at those skills.

751
01:08:57,966 --> 01:09:06,480
But of course, you need results and you might need to hide that work for a bit before you actually reveal it to this is what we've been doing Well, and this this is down to your environment. But yeah.

752
01:09:07,086 --> 01:09:10,480
And no one's going to be mad at you if you come back saying, oh, by the way, guys, I also did this.

753
01:09:10,880 --> 01:09:11,620
Yeah, exactly.

